WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
Now we talked about some practical applications of Python as well.

00:05.000 --> 00:08.000
We talked about list comprehension.

00:08.000 --> 00:13.000
Let's say that we wanted to create a list of only odd numbers between zero

00:13.000 --> 00:16.000
and 100 using a list comprehension.

00:16.000 --> 00:25.000
We can do that by using range to get the numbers from zero to 99 in this case

00:26.000 --> 00:30.000
and add them to our list comprehension.

00:30.000 --> 00:35.000
Only if that number that is generated is divisible by two.

00:35.000 --> 00:42.000
You can also use a set comprehension or a dictionary comprehension.

00:42.000 --> 00:45.000
Those are outside of the scope of the course,

00:45.000 --> 00:49.000
but they are included on the course website for you to look at.

00:49.000 --> 00:54.000
Slicing is another important concept in Python.

00:55.000 --> 01:00.000
Let's say I had a list that looked like this with individual characters in it.

01:00.000 --> 01:02.000
I can take a look at the length.

01:02.000 --> 01:05.000
I could also ask for slices of my list.

01:05.000 --> 01:15.000
So for example, the slice from four to six.

01:15.000 --> 01:19.000
If I wanted to exclude that value,

01:19.000 --> 01:22.000
I would get the same results because by default,

01:22.000 --> 01:26.000
if you don't provide the value, it goes all the way to the end.

01:26.000 --> 01:30.000
If you don't provide a start value, it goes all the way to the beginning.

01:30.000 --> 01:36.000
Passing in no arguments at all is a quick shortcut to get a copy of a list in Python.

01:36.000 --> 01:44.000
You can also use the zip function to take two lists and combine them together.

01:44.000 --> 01:50.000
This is especially useful when you are converting between types.

01:50.000 --> 01:57.000
So I recommend that you go through the converting between types exercise on your own when you have time.

